Adventures in Technology
March 3, 2014	· by narcolapser	· Bookmark the permalink. ·

IMG_0146I’ve shared about some of the outreaches we have done here and my missions work with the dance team and on my own; but I haven’t really talked about my computer work here. Part of the reason for that is some of it is hard to explain without going into technical details, but it is none the less part of God’s work for me here and is interesting to me. So here’s a glimpse of some of what I’ve been up to.

Attached is a picture of me working on our leadership laptop.  One day when the leadership team was going to have a meeting they grabbed the laptop they use for minutes and opened it up getting ready to go ….and there was nothing. The computer didn’t boot up. The other two people in the IT office, Kees-Jan and Celia, took a look at it and didn’t find the problem. So I decided to take a whack at it. Since I knew my colleagues knew their stuff, I didn’t see any point in re-trying what they had already done. The laptop was dead so far as we could establish so with nothing to lose, I took it apart.

Maybe a cable is loose inside. Maybe a part has fallen out. Maybe there will be a problem that we can’t see outside but when I open it all the way, take it apart, piece by piece, the problem will just suddenly be obvious. That was my hope. So I sat like a mad scientist or an electronics equivalent of a surgeon; surrounded with the pieces of the broken machine scattered around me.

An hour or two later I emerge from the operating room with the diagnosis. The CPU had been fried and the heat sink showed signs of scorching. So I acquired a new processor for 12 pounds and replaced it. Another two hours in the electronics “O.R.” and out emerged a healthy, functional laptop. Everyone was happy.

I have had a number of things that I have worked on here to help keep the Lifehope ministries running smoothly and efficiently. Fixing the leadership laptop was one of the shorter projects I’ve worked on, but was probably one of the most satisfying. Being handed a machine that doesn’t work, doing a really involved repair, and then handing back a laptop that works just fine was exciting. A number of my other projects were not exactly less exciting, but exciting in a different way because the results were not as direct.

One of the first projects I was working on when I got here was an upgrade on our network fire wall. As with much of the rest of the world we have a limited internet connection. Works great when there is no one on it, but when 45 people are all using the internet at once, it feels like dial-up. So we tried to use the firewall to get it to run as smoothly as possible.  We had a few people comment to us when it was fixed that the internet seemed so much better, and that is always good to hear.

The next project is one that is on-going and is my biggest project since I’ve gotten here. There are nearly 200 people at Lifehope, and only 3 computer people to tend to the problems that pop up. So I’ve started working on a system that would take some of the load off of us. It’s called Salt. This software, when I get it ready, will take care of all the computers on the network for us. From making sure software in installed as should be, to installing printers, and turning the machines off at night. Hopefully when done we’ll be able to eliminate problems before they arise, or at very least, fix them very quickly.

In addition to that I have taken care of a number of smaller problems, tending to the copy machine, taking care of our conference room, tidying up in the IT office, etc. One slightly nerve-racking one that has been assigned to me is to compile a policy on when and how we should buy new equipment and when and how we should retire old equipment. Which brings me to prayer requests – please pray for:

    Wisdom as I work out the equipment policies. I’m honored to have been entrusted with something as important as this, but certainly want to seek God’s guidance to create a policy that works well for Lifehope.
    My Salt project as I work towards getting that up and running well soon; that God would give me the insight to know how to get over the hurdles that keep it from being a functional system.
    The equipment here at Lifehope as well. God has met our needs and blessed us with some good equipment.  Pray that we can take good care of it and that it will serve us for a long time.

God bless,

Toby
